{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>Cast it to the ground, and he cast it to the ground and it became a serpent.</b> You should contemplate that the change of nature, the transformation of the staff into a serpent, was by the power of reversing the letters, and behold, this is alluded to in the verse; and when the letters returned to their original state the serpent turned back into a staff, and this is what is written: ״ויהי למטה בכפו״ — and it became a staff in his palm. Now you already know that the root of the special Name is three letters, for the heh is doubled — and understand this." "<b>And there is</b> to interpret that in these two signs — that is, the sign of the staff and the sign of the leprous affliction — the Holy One, blessed be He, alluded to him that it is in His hand to give life to the dead and to put the living to death: for the staff was dry wood, like a dead thing, and it became a living serpent; and his hand, which was alive, became as though dead, since leprous affliction (צרעת) is an illness considered like death, in the manner of what is written (Numbers 12:12): ״אל נא תהי כמת״. And these two signs He had him perform both in the wilderness and in Egypt for Israel's sake, as HaShem said to him concerning the sign of the serpent: ״למען יאמינו כי נראה אליך HaShem״, and it is written: ״והיה אם לא יאמינו לקול האות הראשון״ — and if they do not believe the voice of the first sign — ״והאמינו לקול האות האחרון״ — and they will believe the voice of the latter sign; ״והיה אם לא יאמינו גם לשני האותיות האלה״ — and it shall be if they do not believe even these two signs, etc. And the fact that he needed to perform them in the wilderness is something to wonder at — why was this necessary, since Moses did not lack faith in the word of HaShem, and why should he need to perform them before reaching Israel in Egypt? And the answer to this is what our Rabbis of blessed memory received: that these two signs, when he was commanded to perform them in the wilderness for himself — the reason for the first was to allude to him that he had spoken slander against Israel, when he said ״והן לא יאמינו לי ולא ישמעו בקולי״ — 'and they will not believe me and will not heed my voice' — after the Holy One, blessed be He, had told him ״ושמעו לקולך״ — 'and they will heed your voice'; and the second was to punish him for it. Therefore he needed to perform them twice — in the wilderness, for himself, on account of the slander and on account of his punishment; and in Egypt, for Israel, so that they would believe."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 4, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:56:05.836867983Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}